title: Basalt-Derived Calcium for Lower Carbon Portland Cement
authors: &id001
- family-names: Pierce
  given-names: Brad
  orcid: https://orcid.org/0009-0009-7427-2969
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23547373
date-released: '2026-10-21'
url: https://zenodo.org/records/23547373
abstract: Basalt-derived calcium warrants integrated development toward 10–20% lower
  total emissions from Portland cement manufacture. The route avoids carbonate decomposition
  but must pay for extraction, separation and regeneration. We combine a coupled operating
  specification with a paired carbon budget, assigning zero coproduct, carbon-removal
  or allocation credits and no assumed kiln-energy saving. A derived reagent-equivalent
  constraint accounts explicitly for sodium release; the principal selected multiplier
  is 1.30 relative to calcium-only demand. Under a reconstructed dry-process comparator
  with the same energy supply and matched cement, selected operation at 20–40 g CO₂e/kWh
  calculates 12.5–19.4% lower total emissions. At 30 g/kWh, the conventional and basalt
  cases are 756.9 and 636.0 kg CO₂e/t. At 50 g/kWh the reduction falls to 9.1%, and
  combined process penalties can erase the benefit. These conditional results justify
  testing whether selective calcium recovery, representative-liquor recycling and
  heat/water integration can coexist in a circuit that produces qualified cement.
  An instrumented demonstration with complete matched inventories should establish
  the achievable reduction.
